{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Wandered.</b> שִׁיּוּט is nothing but a term of strolling, אישבנ״ייר — without effort." "<b>And ground it in millstones etc.</b> It did not descend as hand-mill and not in a pot and not in a mortar, but its taste would change for the ground and for the pounded and for the cooked (Sifrei)." "<b>In a pot.</b> A cauldron." "<b>The cream of oil.</b> The exudation of oil — so Dunash explained it, and similar to it: ״נֶהְפַּךְ לְשַׁדִּי בְּחַרְבֹנֵי קַיִץ״ (Psalms 32) — and the lamed is the root: נֶהְפַּךְ לִחְלוּחִי בְּחַרְבוֹנֵי קַיִץ. And our Rabbis explained it as a term for breasts, but the matter of breasts is irrelevant to oil. And one cannot say לְשַׁד הַשָּׁמֶן is from the language of ״וַיִּשְׁמַן יְשֻׁרוּן״ (Deuteronomy 32), for if so the mem would be vowelized with qamatz and its accent below the mem; now that the mem is vowelized with small patach and the accent is under the shin, it is a term for oil, and the shin is vowelized with qamatz gadol and is not vowelized with small patach because it is the end of the verse. Another interpretation: לְשַׁד is notarikon — לִישׁ שֶׁמֶן דְּבַשׁ, like a dough kneaded with oil and coated with honey. And the Targum of Onkelos, who translates ״דְּלִישׁ בְּמִשְׁחָא,״ inclines toward Dunash's interpretation — for a dough kneaded with oil has in it an exudation of oil."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "the la'az is Old French esbanoir, 'to amuse oneself'"}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:10:24.999789180Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}